 "music": "bed",
 "title": "Where did the money go?",
 "sources": [
  "PostgreSQL tutorial, 3.4 Transactions (https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial-transactions.html, fetched 2026-11-24): a payment from Alice's account to Bob's is several UPDATE steps; 'if something goes wrong partway through the operation, none of the steps executed so far will take effect'; 'bundles multiple steps into a single, all-or-nothing operation'; BEGIN ... COMMIT; ROLLBACK cancels all updates so far (example: noticing Alice's balance went negative); updates are 'logged in permanent storage (i.e., on disk) before the transaction is reported complete'.",
  "Illustration only: the 100 rupee transfer is an example; real payment systems add more layers than one database transaction."
 ],
 "lines": [
  {
   "text": "A hundred rupees leaves your account, the server crashes, and it never arrives. Lost, refunded, or never left?",
   "caps": "*₹100 LEAVES | SERVER *CRASHES | LOST OR *NEVER LEFT?"
  },
  {
   "text": "A transfer is two steps: subtract from you, then add to them.",
   "caps": "*TWO STEPS | *SUBTRACT | THEN *ADD"
  },
  {
   "text": "Crash between them, and without a transaction, the money simply vanishes.",
   "caps": "*CRASH BETWEEN | MONEY *VANISHES"
  },
  {
   "text": "So you wrap both steps in a transaction: begin, both updates, commit.",
   "caps": "A *TRANSACTION | *BEGIN | *COMMIT"
  },
  {
   "text": "It's all or nothing. If anything fails halfway, none of the steps take effect.",
   "caps": "*ALL OR NOTHING | FAILS *HALFWAY | *NONE OF IT"
  },
  {
   "text": "And once it's committed, it's written to disk, so even a crash can't lose it.",
   "caps": "*COMMITTED | WRITTEN TO *DISK | SURVIVES A *CRASH"
  },
  {
   "text": "Spot a problem midway, like a negative balance? Rollback cancels every update so far.",
   "caps": "*NEGATIVE BALANCE? | *ROLLBACK | ALL *CANCELLED"
  },
  {
   "text": "So with a transaction, the money never left. Did you guess right?",
   "caps": "IT *NEVER LEFT | DID YOU GUESS *RIGHT?"
  }
 ],
